<?xml version="1.0"?>
<psalm
	errorLevel="4"
	phpVersion="8.2"
	strictBinaryOperands="false"
	resolveFromConfigFile="true"
	findUnusedBaselineEntry="true"
	findUnusedCode="false"
	findUnusedIssueHandlerSuppression="false"
	xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
	xmlns="https://getpsalm.org/schema/config"
	xsi:schemaLocation="https://getpsalm.org/schema/config vendor/vimeo/psalm/config.xsd"
>
	<!--
		lib/ is clean at errorLevel 4, so there is no baseline file here to go stale.
		Level 3 would add 39 findings, 29 of them in ImageWatermarker: unchecked
		imagecreatefrom*() / imagecolorallocate() returns, which are `false` and `null` on
		failure and are then passed straight on. They are a real backlog, tracked in
		doc/tasks.md instead of frozen into a baseline — a baseline would turn the gate
		green while every one of them is still there.
	-->

	<!--
		Only lib/ is analysed. The test suite is not: it is built almost entirely out of
		PHPUnit mock objects, whose types Psalm cannot narrow, so analysing it would bury
		the findings that matter under intersection-type noise.
	-->
	<projectFiles>
		<directory name="lib" />
		<ignoreFiles>
			<directory name="vendor" />
		</ignoreFiles>
	</projectFiles>

	<!--
		The hand-written declarations of Nextcloud *server* classes that lib/ is written
		against, fed to Psalm as the exact same files the test suite loads — one source of
		truth, rather than a second set of stubs free to drift from the first:

		- tests/stubs/CoreStubs.php holds OCA\DAV\Connector\Sabre\{Node,File,Directory},
		  OC\Streamer and the two events, transcribed from a real server and not
		  installable from packagist. Psalm still cannot tell whether the stub matches
		  core — only a diff against a real server can, see the header of that file — but
		  it now checks that lib/Dav/ matches the stub, which no tool did before.
		- tests/bootstrap.php declares OC\Hooks\Emitter, which OCP\Files\IRootFolder
		  extends and nextcloud/ocp does not ship.

		Both must be <stubs> and not <extraFiles>: every declaration in them is wrapped in
		an `if (!class_exists(...))` guard, and Psalm's scanner only registers conditional
		declarations from stub files. As extraFiles they are silently scanned to nothing
		and every symbol comes back undefined.
	-->
	<stubs>
		<file name="tests/stubs/CoreStubs.php" />
		<file name="tests/bootstrap.php" />
	</stubs>

	<!--
		Scanned for declarations but never analysed. Psalm resolves everything else
		(sabre/dav, tc-lib-pdf, doctrine/dbal, psr/*) through Composer's autoloader;
		nextcloud/ocp is the exception, because it ships no autoload section at all, so
		nothing can find OCP\* or NCU\* by class name. Pointing Psalm at it is the whole
		point of the exercise: it is the typed declaration of core's public API, and it is
		what makes lib/ checked against core rather than against itself.
	-->
	<extraFiles>
		<directory name="vendor/nextcloud/ocp/OCP" />
		<directory name="vendor/nextcloud/ocp/NCU" />
	</extraFiles>

	<issueHandlers>
		<!--
			#[\Override] is PHP 8.3, and this app supports 8.2 (composer.json, and both are
			in the CI matrix). PHP ignores unknown attributes, so adding them would not be
			fatal on 8.2 — but it would put a version marker on 29 methods for a naming
			convention, not a type check. Revisit when the floor moves to 8.3.
		-->
		<MissingOverrideAttribute errorLevel="suppress" />

		<!--
			ext-imagick is optional (GD is the default engine — see the header of the file),
			so it is not in composer.json's require and is not loaded on every machine that
			runs Psalm. Psalm reflects extension classes from the *running* PHP, and there
			is no maintained stub package to stand in for it, so `new \Imagick()` reads as
			undefined on a host without the extension and as the real class on a host with
			it. Suppressed rather than baselined so both hosts reach the same verdict.

			Both CI runners *do* load imagick — they run the test suite too — so the calls
			in that branch are analysed there. All this silences is the "class does not
			exist" noise on a machine that has no Imagick to check against in the first
			place. Scoped to the one file that touches it, so a typo'd class name anywhere
			else is still an error.

			`findUnusedIssueHandlerSuppression="false"` above is the consequence of exactly
			this: on a host that has the extension nothing throws UndefinedClass here, and
			Psalm's default is to report the suppression itself as unused — so the config
			written to make the two hosts agree would instead fail on one of them. There are
			two suppressions in this file and both are deliberate, which is the whole of
			what that check could tell us.
		-->
		<UndefinedClass>
			<errorLevel type="suppress">
				<file name="lib/Service/ImageWatermarker.php" />
			</errorLevel>
		</UndefinedClass>
	</issueHandlers>
</psalm>
